AB  - The quality of Indonesian cocoa beans is deemed inferior due to inadequate fermentation. Incorporation of microbial starters during fermentation, utilising immobilised cells on gluten pellets, may enhance fermentation efficiency. This study assessed the efficacy of immobilised Pichia kudriavzevii UNJCC Y-137, Lactobacillus plantarum NP-57, and a combination of both on gluten pellets at three immersion durations (3, 6, and 9 h) on the physicochemical properties and volatile compounds of fermented cacao beans. The greatest viability of P. kudriavzevii and L. plantarum on gluten pellets was 6.67 × 10⁸ ± 4.7 × 10⁷ CFU/mL and 2.52 × 10⁹ ± 1.3 × 10⁸ CFU/mL, respectively after 9 h immersion. Scanning electron microscope analysis confirmed successful immobilisation after 9 h of soaking. The combination of P. kudriavzevii and L. plantarum yielded superior fermentation quality, achieving a fermentation percentage of 85.67 ± 2.73% and antioxidant activity of 86.04 ± 0.16%. The volatile compounds on cocoa beans comprised organic acids, alcohols, aldehydes, amines, alkaloids, esters, and ketones. In conclusion, the use of immobilised yeast P. kudriavzevii UNJCC Y-137 and bacterium L. plantarum NP-57 on gluten pellets, particularly their combination with extended immersion duration, significantly enhanced fermentation quality, antioxidant activity, and volatile compound profile of cocoa beans.
