AB  - In Malaysia’s agricultural industry, traditional disease detection methods used for cocoa fields present three main challenges: inefficiencies in human labour costs, extended detection periods, and errors made by humans during inspections. The basic disease detection methods in Malaysian cocoa fields have led to the introduction of ineffective disease control, reducing crop volume and product quality. Therefore, we introduced a hybrid model that integrates MobileNetV3 with machine learning classifiers, including support vector machine (SVM), K-nearest neighbour (KNN), random forest (RF), logistic regression (LR), and extreme gradient boosting tree (XGBoost) to enhance disease identification in cocoa plants. MobileNetV3, selected for its efficiency over MobileNetV2, is tailored to Malaysia’s unique agricultural conditions, offering a faster, more cost-effective solution for real-time disease classification. The base machine learning models were evaluated and compared, where the SVM model achieved the highest accuracy of 67.73%, followed closely by the LR model (67.2%) and the XGBoost model (65.87%). Fine-tuning these models resulted in performance improvements, with the SVM model achieving 70.00% accuracy and the XGBoost model increasing to 68.97%. A deep learning-based model using MobileNetV3 demonstrated significant improvements, with the MobileNetV3-Large model achieving an accuracy of 96.8%, outperforming the EfficientNetB0 model (95.4%) and the MobileNetV3-Small model (95.8%). To enhance classification accuracy, we integrated MobileNetV3-Large with traditional machine learning classifiers. The MobileNetV3-Large + XGBoost combination showed the highest performance, with an accuracy of 97.53%, demonstrating its effectiveness in improving disease detection precision. The results indicate that leveraging deep learning alongside machine learning enhances diagnostic accuracy, offering a scalable and locally adapted solution for sustainable cocoa crop management. Further research is necessary for dataset expansion, the optimization of model architectures, implementing computer vision, and real-world deployment to automate precision agriculture.
